## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about end-to-end exactly-once semantics across HTTP, DB, Kafka, and Redis in CNPA stacks, including failure modes and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Exactly-once across services
- Outbox pattern and transactional boundaries
- Deduplication using client_request_id
- Idempotent sinks/consumers and cache invalidation
- Observability: duplicates, latency, retry rates

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: HTTP handler scaffold
async function handleOrder(req, res) {
  const id = req.headers['client_request_id'];
  if (!id) return res.status(400).send('missing id');
  await db.transaction(async (tx) => {
    await tx.query('INSERT INTO orders (...) VALUES (...);');
    await tx.query('INSERT INTO dedupe (client_request_id) VALUES (?) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;', [id]);
    await tx.query('INSERT INTO outbox (order_id, event) VALUES (...);');
  });
  res.status(200).send({ status: 'accepted' });
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate idempotent consumer behavior under replay scenarios?
- What metrics and alarms would you configure to detect dedupe or latency anomalies?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical debugging across HTTP, DB, Kafka, and Redis in a realistic CNPA flow, focusing on end-to-end latency and data correctness during policy changes.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end latency measurement across services
- Distributed tracing and per-tenant sampling
- Kafka producer/consumer offset lag and acks
- Transactional outbox and idempotent sinks
- Backpressure, batching, and read-after-write verification

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo transactional outbox
db.transaction(async (tx) => {
  await tx.insert('audit', auditRec);
  await tx.insert('outbox', { ...auditRec, event: 'AUDIT_LOG' });
  // commit happens here
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you distinguish tenant-specific lag from global system lag?
- What config changes to Kafka/SRE tooling help monitor and mitigate this without harming durability?

## Why This Is Asked

Real-world CNPA stacks face subtle, cross-component bottlenecks during feature flag rollouts. Isolating whether latency comes from cache invalidation, the indexing pipeline, or rollout logic is critical for safe canary deployments.

## Key Concepts

- Outbox pattern and atomic writes across DB and messaging
- Idempotent indexing/upserts in Elasticsearch
- Cache invalidation correctness and TTL strategies
- End-to-end tracing across HTTP, DB, Kafka, Redis, ES
- Canary deployments and feature flag rollout risk controls

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: idempotent index update for Elasticsearch
async function upsertIndex(docId, doc) {
  // upsert via Elasticsearch; idempotent on duplicates
  await es.update({
    index: 'pricing',
    id: docId,
    body: { doc, doc_as_upsert: true }
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate canary rollout safety with metrics and rollback criteria?
- What instrumentation would you add to visualize end-to-end latency across CNPA components in production?

## Why This Is Asked
Explain flag-driven, safe rollouts across CNPA boundaries.

## Key Concepts
- Feature flags, schema evolution, backward/forward compatibility, idempotent writes.
- Outbox pattern, nullable fields, partial rollouts.
- Observability: metrics for latency, error, cohort analytics.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node: read flag, set tenant_id only for flagged users
if (flagEnabled(user)) {
  payload.tenant_id = user.tenantId
} else {
  payload.tenant_id = null
}
// write to DB and enqueue to Kafka with the same payload
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to handle schema migrations without downtime?
- How to test rollback in CI/CD?

## Why This Is Asked

Observability is critical for CNPA; end-to-end tracing helps identify latency sources across HTTP, DB, and Kafka.

## Key Concepts

- Distributed tracing
- OpenTelemetry SDKs and exporters
- Trace context propagation headers and Kafka headers
- Sampling strategies
- End-to-end verification under load

## Code Example

```javascript
// Node.js OpenTelemetry setup sketch
const tracer = opentelemetry.trace.getTracer('cnpa');
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const span = tracer.startSpan('http', { attributes: { 'http.method': req.method }});
  res.on('finish', () => span.end());
  next();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you ensure producer retries don’t split traces?
- How would you visualize traces and correlate with latency metrics?
